From 4764cff9a633c83a803738ee4c266b6a5a4f6d79 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <jelon@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 13:11:18 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 053/206] feat(shorebird): ResumeOnSimulator, sharing the
 simulator-entry sequence

Nine of sixteen. 66 instructions / 264 bytes, matching theirs, 56 of 66 words
byte-identical with the other ten differing only in a Thread displacement.

All but six of those instructions are the same sequence
TransitionDartToSimulatorIfNeeded already emits, so it is now
GenerateEnterSimulator, parameterised on two things their two callers differ in:
the SimulatorExecutionSource (0 for the transition stub, 1 here) and whether
Simulator::pc_ is stored once or twice. The duplicate store is theirs -- the
transition stub's generator writes it and RunOnSimulator writes it again -- and
a version that stored it once would be four bytes short.

The CPU->Sim body still compares 60/68 after the refactor, so it changed no
bytes, and both tests now guard the shared helper.

On the negative control, which failed to run twice before it ran. First attempt:
the sabotage patch asserted on a string that had been reflowed. Second attempt:
the line number came from a command substitution whose grep returned nothing, so
python got a garbage argument and never patched, while the following "restored
exit=0" printed exactly as it does on success. Third attempt works and prints an
explicit PATCH_OK before building. Perturbing the shared helper's source
argument turns both StubCode_ShorebirdResumeOnSimulatorMatchesTheirBytes and
StubCode_ShorebirdCpuToSimBodyMatchesTheirBytes to exit 255, and reverting
returns both to 0.

Verified: 86 shorebird cases pass, both builds link, verify_reproduction.sh
reports 24 artifacts with 0 failures.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

